bug_descriptions |bug_id, line_num, content|
    seed_typed_f64_option_some_heap_allocates_per_some_never_released_2026-11-27, 0, "Owner decision 2026-11-27: typed f64?/Option<f64> = tag plus the exact 64-bit payload inline, no heap allocation. Seed today: every explicit Some(x) is rt_enum_new (a 32-byte RuntimeEnum registered through track_transient_heap, codegen/instr/result.rs create_enum_value; LLVM functions.rs OptionSome likewise) and the flat form (return x into an f64? slot) heap-boxes a HeapFloat (rt_value_float). Measured aarch64, seeds e2479b4127a0a3a9 (base) and the exactness fix of seed_jit_explicit_f64_option_some_typed_readers_lose_low_mantissa_bits_2026-11-27: a 4e7 construct + ?? + unwrap loop holds 1.94 GB max RSS (about 48 B per Some), identical on both, never released. An inline form needs a two-word value (tag + payload; no 64-bit NaN-box can carry every f64 plus None) through MIR locals, struct fields, [f64?] element storage, params and the return ABI in cranelift and LLVM and the C runtime. The pure-Simple compiler (src/compiler/50.mir switch_operators_calls.spl bitcast_float_enum_payload / ensure_option_handle) also uses the heap rt_enum_new handle for every Optional. Not attempted in the exactness lane."
    seed_typed_f64_option_some_heap_allocates_per_some_never_released_2026-11-27, 1, "PARTIAL 2026-11-27 (work/f64-option-inline-layout-20261127): typed f64? / Option<f64> LOCALS are an inline (tag, bits) pair in the seed (mir/float_option_inline.rs, one MIR pass at the lower_module choke point, so cranelift JIT, native archive and LLVM share it; tag 0 nil, 4 None enum, 1 Some raw-bits enum, 2 flat float, 3 Some over a boxed float, presence = tag & 3). Some(x), a flat x, nil/None and local copies write the pair with no runtime call; ??, if val, match, .unwrap(), == nil read it with no runtime call; any other consumer gets the one-word form back from rt_option_float_pack at that read; a legacy word entering a local is unpacked by rt_option_float_tag + rt_option_float_bits (no allocation). New rt_option_float_tag / rt_option_float_pack in value/objects.rs and src/runtime/runtime_native.c. Measured aarch64, base seed dc41771399af764f vs fix seed dd4adb4888424f68, 4 alternated rounds: the 4e7 Some + ?? + unwrap loop holds 214 MB instead of 2044 MB peak RSS and runs 0.92 s instead of 12.8 s; a local with an escaping read keeps its one-word form in a third hidden slot written at every store (same box count and lifetime as the one-word slot; a lazily built word was freed with the transient scope of its first read), so a value is boxed once per assignment (a loop-invariant local passed to a function 2e7 times grows 0 KB); peak RSS no longer grows between 2e6 and 4e7 iterations (grew 1.85 GB). STILL BOXED, so this stays open: function returns and parameters (one-I64 ABI in both backends, cranelift locals are not addressable so no out-pointer), struct fields (field_index * 8 hardcoded in 8 lowering sites plus runtime introspection), [f64?] elements (runtime word arrays), closure captures (8-byte I64 capture slots, the outliner binds captures through the parent local index), locals captured by a lambda, f32?. A Some stored into one of those still allocates one rt_enum_new or rt_value_float object, freed only at transient scope / session end. Spec test/01_unit/language/f64_option_inline_layout_spec.spl; Rust tests mir::float_option_inline::tests and native_inline_f64_option_carriers_and_readers_are_exact_against_c_runtime."

